Diane turned. “Keep that device stationary.”

The assistant stood in the alcove doorway with the flashlight in her hand. “It went out.”

The beam vanished.

Evan pulled the meter back before Abby reached eight.

“Hold position,” Diane said.

“It failed again,” the assistant called.

Evan turned toward the hall. “That is two devices.”

“The meter remains on,” Abby said.

The green indicator still glowed.

Diane led them back to the alcove. The assistant pressed the flashlight switch. Nothing happened. She pressed again and shook it once.

“Stop handling it,” Diane said.

The assistant froze.

Abby moved in close. “Flashlight failure at four-twenty-one. Operator removed it from the bench before failure entered frame.”

“I picked it up because Miriam said it was closer,” the assistant said.

“When was the switch pressed?”

“After they left.”

“Why?”

“I wanted light in the hall.”

“Was there light?”

The assistant nodded. “For a few seconds.”

Abby looked at Diane. “The activation is outside my frame.”

Evan said, “But we all saw the beam.”

“We saw a beam cross the wall,” Diane said. “We lack the switch sequence and handling.”

The assistant’s face tightened. “I am not lying.”

“No one accused you of lying.” Diane held out her hand. “Set it on the towel.”

The assistant placed it beside the spare batteries.

Evan kept filming. “Every time the equipment reacts, you take it away.”

“Every time its state becomes uncertain, I stop additional handling.” Diane took custody of both devices after Evan broke the count and the flashlight changed operators outside coverage.

He accused her of dismantling the only sequence they had. Miriam answered that Diane was recording how the sequence occurred. Diane placed the meter and flashlight on the towel. Miriam said something remained in the corridor, then accepted that the image, meter, and flashlight retained ordinary failure paths.

Diane opened the flashlight’s battery cap over the towel. One cell dropped into her palm. The second remained lodged inside the tube. She tilted the body beneath Abby’s light. The lower battery slid free only after she tapped the flashlight once against her gloved hand.

A narrow strip of torn paper clung near the spring.

“What is that?” the assistant asked.

Diane lifted it with tweezers. The fragment appeared to be part of a battery wrapper, folded against the contact.

“Possible interruption,” Diane said. “We need the original battery orientation and a continuity test.”

Evan turned his camera toward the fragment. “Possible.”

“Yes.”

“You always have a possible explanation.”

“I have a visible obstruction.”

Miriam faced the corridor again.

Abby’s camera light caught a pale shape crossing between Miriam and the doorway. It moved downward in a slow curve and disappeared near the floor.

The assistant gasped. “What was that?”

Evan swung his camera too late.

Abby kept hers steady. “Something crossed close to the lens.”

“An orb?” Evan asked.

“A pale out-of-focus object.”

“Replay it.”

“After the devices are secured.”

“Abby.”

She looked at Diane.

Diane sealed the loose batteries and torn wrapper in separate evidence sleeves. “Replay the last ten seconds without enlarging or enhancing.”

Abby opened the clip on her camera screen. The group gathered close enough that their coats touched.

The shape entered at the upper left, soft-edged and almost transparent. It widened as it crossed the frame, then vanished below Miriam’s elbow. The background showed no distinct object along its apparent path.

“Slow it down,” Evan said.

“Normal speed first,” Abby replied.

They watched again.

On the third play, Diane saw a thin bright strand at the edge of the frame a fraction before the larger blur appeared. It could have hung from Abby’s glove, Miriam’s sleeve, the blanket, or the frayed insulation carried from the crawlspace. The screen provided no depth.

“Near the lens,” Diane said.

“You cannot know that,” Evan replied.

“It expands while the background stays sharp.”

“Or it moved toward us.”

“Both fit the image.”

Miriam studied the replay. “I felt pressure at the same moment.”

Abby checked the audio waveform. “You turned toward the corridor half a second before the object entered frame.”

Evan looked at Miriam. “You sensed it coming.”

Miriam watched the small screen once more. “Or I heard clothing move behind me.”

The assistant shifted the blanket away from her coat. Several pale fibers clung to the wool.

Diane pointed. “Record those before anyone brushes them off.”

Evan’s mouth tightened. “You think her blanket caused it.”

“I think fibers are present near the camera.”

A sound seemed to come from the classroom-storage room.

It resembled one long breath drawn close to a microphone: a rough intake, a pause, then air released through a narrow opening.

The assistant gripped Miriam’s sleeve.

Evan ran into the corridor.

“Stop,” Diane called.

He crossed the floor joint, camera raised. Abby followed to keep him in frame. Miriam moved after them with her assistant close behind.

Diane stayed long enough to close the evidence sleeves and place both failed devices in her facilities case.

The breath-like sound came again.

This time it seemed to end with a faint scrape against the other side of the classroom-storage door.

Diane snapped the case shut and entered the corridor.

On the workbench behind her, the flashlight remained open, its contact test unfinished.

## Scene 4 — The Student They Build

Evan reached the classroom-storage door before Diane caught up with him.

He put one hand on the lever.

“Leave it.”

The second breath ended behind the painted steel, followed by a light scrape at knee height. Evan’s hand stayed on the lever. Abby stood two steps behind him with her camera centered on his wrist, the latch, and the strip of floor beneath the door. Miriam and her assistant waited at the nearest wall joint. Diane came between Evan and the frame.

“State the time,” she said.

Abby read it from the synchronized display.

Diane listened. Air moved through the corridor supply duct. Water crossed above the ceiling in the same copper line that had carried the metallic strike. From the room came no further scrape.

“Could be someone inside,” Evan said.

“The room was locked before your arrival.”

“So unlock it.”

Diane took the key ring from beneath her coat and showed the classroom-storage key to Abby’s lens. She inserted it without turning.

“Current positions,” she said.

Abby named each person and their distance from the door. Evan shifted his camera toward the narrow pane of wired glass. The room beyond remained dark except for a green exit sign reflected from the corridor.

Diane turned the key. The latch released. She pulled the door toward herself a few inches and stopped.

A draft crossed her knuckles.

The pressure came from a return grille high on the opposite wall. A sheet of paper stirred somewhere near the floor, then settled.

“No one enters until I clear the room.”

Evan raised his camera over her shoulder.

Diane widened the opening and switched on the wall lights. Two fluorescent rows blinked, brightened, and left the rear corners in gray shadow. The room had once served as a small classroom. The district now used it for academic storage: stacked tablet-arm chairs, rolling whiteboards, boxes of retired workbooks, plastic bins of stage props, and three dented filing cabinets waiting for disposal. A teacher’s desk stood beneath the return grille. The paper Diane had heard lay half beneath it.

She scanned the floor, the space behind the door, and the aisle between the chairs. No person occupied the room. The lower scrape had come from a loose rubber door sweep dragging across the threshold when pressure shifted the door.

“Clear,” she said. “Abby first. Continuous shot.”

Abby entered and filmed the room clockwise. Diane followed. Evan came next, then Miriam and her assistant. Diane kept the door open against the wall and marked its position with blue tape.

The assistant looked toward the teacher’s desk. “That paper moved.”

“Air from the return,” Diane said.

Miriam stood in the center aisle with her hands at her sides. She looked from the cabinets to the stacked chairs, then to a row of old composition books on an open shelf.

Evan asked, “Is this where the closer one went?”

Miriam waited before answering. “I feel someone younger here.”

Diane pressed the marker button on her recorder.

“Human?” Evan asked.

“I think so.”

“Student?”

Miriam faced the stacked chairs. “School belongs to the impression, but I cannot tell whether this person studied in this room.”

Evan stepped around Abby to keep Miriam’s face in frame. “What happened to them?”

Diane said, “Observation before biography.”

“I asked a question.”

“You supplied a category.”

Miriam turned toward Diane. “She is right. I have an impression of youth, school, and distress. Nothing more specific yet.”

Her assistant moved closer to her.

Miriam closed her eyes. The room’s return fan drew air through the grille in a steady rush. The fluorescent ballast above the cabinets produced a thin, high vibration. Diane watched Miriam’s hands. The right thumb rubbed once across the first joint of her index finger.

“Someone tried to disappear while remaining in the room,” Miriam said.

Evan lowered his voice. “Bullied?”

“I see a person looking down while other people wait for an answer.”

“Called on in class,” the assistant said.

Miriam opened her eyes. “That fits the posture.”

Diane pressed the marker again. “The assistant supplied the classroom interpretation.”

The assistant looked toward her. “I was only clarifying.”

“I am preserving the sequence.”

Miriam walked toward the old composition books without touching them. “The distress centers on being unable to answer fast enough. Words arrive, then break apart before they can be spoken.”

“Speech problem?” Evan asked.

“Possibly. It may also be anxiety, hearing, attention, reading, or the way this person remembers the moment.”

“You hear a voice?”

“Fragments. The reception comes and goes.”

Evan glanced at his camera monitor. “Like interference.”

“That is your comparison.”

Miriam nodded toward him. “Yes.”

A dry rustle came from beneath the teacher’s desk.

Everyone turned.

The loose paper lifted at one corner, dragged a short distance across the tile, and stopped against a chair leg. Abby kept filming. Diane crossed the room and crouched beside it.

“Airflow increased when the door opened,” she said. “No one touch the paper.”

The page had been folded twice. Pencil marks showed through the back. A purple line ran along one edge where a copier had cut off a school letterhead. Diane photographed it in place with the facilities tablet, then filmed her gloved hand unfolding it.

Three sentences occupied the upper half:

I tried to tell you.

I lose the words when everybody looks.

Please stop making me do it in front of them.

The lower half contained faint printed prompts under the handwriting: WHAT HAPPENED? WHAT WERE YOU MISSING? WHAT COULD AN ADULT HAVE CHANGED?

Evan leaned into Abby’s frame. “That is exactly what she described.”

“The page is an exercise sheet,” Diane said.

“It is still here.”

“So are several hundred old workbooks.”

The assistant covered her mouth with one hand. “That child was humiliated.”

“We have no child attached to this page,” Diane said. “No date, name, class, or proof that the handwriting describes an event.”

Miriam crouched at the opposite side of the paper. She kept both hands on her knees.

“May I read it?” she asked.

“From there.”

Miriam read the three handwritten lines silently. At the second sentence, the muscles around her eyes tightened, and she inhaled through her nose.

Evan asked, “Does it belong to the person you sensed?”

“I cannot establish ownership.”

“But it matches.”

“It matches part of what I described after we entered a room full of school materials.”

